## Overview

Knoweb3 is a concept for a mobile learning app. It aims to raise Web3 literacy and awareness, and to make learning online a little more interesting than reading one article after another. We designed it as a student project at IIT Guwahati over four weeks, as a team of three, and took it from research through to high-fidelity screens.

{% stats %}
{% stat value="4 weeks" label="Research to final UI" /%}
{% stat value="3" label="People on the team" /%}
{% stat value="2" label="Personas" /%}
{% /stats %}

## The problem

Web3 is the idea of a next version of the web built on blockchain, with decentralisation and token-based economics at its core. Understanding it lets people take part in, and have a say in, technology that is starting to shape their lives. A lot of capital is flowing into the space, yet a large share of people are either unaware of it or carry misconceptions about it.

Learning it on your own is also hard. Information is scattered across many sites, it often contradicts itself, and the jargon puts people off early. Online learning in general has a retention problem too: the sources we read pointed to high drop-out rates for online courses.

So the question we set ourselves was how to give a beginner one reliable place to learn Web3, and a reason to keep going after the first few days.

## My role

I worked on this with Harinandh V R and Harinandanan K M. The project covered user research, UX design and UI design, and the three of us worked through each stage together: the literature review, survey and interviews, personas and journey mapping, prioritisation, information architecture, wireframes, the visual language and the final screens. We worked in Figma and Photoshop.

## Process

We followed a double diamond: discover and define for research, then develop and deliver for design.

### Discover

We started with a literature review of the Web3 space and how informed people were about it. Awareness skewed young and male, and a large share of US consumers had never heard the term. On the learning side, mobile was the main device for online learning among students in India, and learning apps retained people noticeably worse than classrooms.

Next we ran an online survey to see how well different age groups understood Web3 and how they usually go about learning something new.

![Survey results on age, learning habits and familiarity with Web3 terms](/work/knoweb3/survey-results.webp "Survey results: learning habits and familiarity with Web3 terms")

Most learning-app users in the survey were 16 to 30 and found those apps effective. They preferred video, and studying alone over groups or a mentor. Almost half had heard the term "Web3" but weren't sure what it meant, while nearly 20% hadn't heard it at all. Most had heard words like blockchain, NFT and metaverse without a clear idea of what they were. 60.8% felt a learning app could organise and track their learning better. Many also said they stop learning something once it feels too difficult.

We then interviewed a small group of students aged 18 to 22 about how they understood Web3, how they learn new things, and where that process breaks down.

{% callout %}
"I find it difficult to choose from the plethora of information sources and websites that come up when I search for something to learn online." One of the students we interviewed
{% /callout %}

Others mentioned feeling left out when friends discuss Web3, distrusting online sources, and getting bored of apps that don't keep them motivated.

Finally, we compared ethereum.org, OdysseyDAO, learnweb3.io, useweb3.xyz and Coursera on features, drawbacks and what we could borrow: community, labelled topics, revision quizzes, level-based starting points, light gamification, progress tracking and offline downloads.

### Define

From the research we built two personas. Akshay is a 19-year-old engineering student who feels left out when his friends talk about Web3 and struggles to finish courses he starts. Nirmal is a 37-year-old product manager who wants reliable, up-to-date information in one place and only has a short window each day to learn.

{% gallery columns=2 %}
![Persona: Akshay Anand, 19, student](/work/knoweb3/persona-akshay.webp)

![Persona: Nirmal Kumar, 37, product manager](/work/knoweb3/persona-nirmal.webp)
{% /gallery %}

A journey map traced what happens when someone tries to learn Web3 online. They hear about it from friends, search, jump between articles, run into contradicting information, and stop. Each stage pointed to an opportunity: an onboarding that keeps early enthusiasm up, a test to find a starting level, universal search, well-grouped content, a community for clearing doubts, and gamification to keep people engaged.

![User journey map with tasks, emotions, pain points and opportunities](/work/knoweb3/user-journey.webp "User journey: from curiosity to giving up")

We listed every feature idea and sorted them with MoSCoW. Must-haves were universal search, reminders, multiple teaching formats, a profile and a dashboard. Should-haves included community peer review, joining DAOs, multi-language support, NFT rewards that can't be sold and wallet connection. We consciously left out an inbuilt IDE, a leaderboard and live sessions to keep the scope focused on beginners.

### Develop

The prioritised features shaped the information architecture: four main sections (Home, Learn, Community and Profile) after sign-up and onboarding. We then mapped the core user flow, from opening the app to choosing a level (or taking a quiz to find it), working through a course, taking a test and redeeming points.

![Information architecture of the app](/work/knoweb3/information-architecture.webp "Information architecture")

![Low-fidelity wireframes](/work/knoweb3/wireframes.webp "Low-fidelity wireframes")

For the visual language, the logo combines a "K" for know, a "W" for web and three strokes for "three". We built the palette around the logo's blue-violet gradient, used SF Pro for type and a simple line icon set.

![Logo development, colour palette, typography and icons](/work/knoweb3/visual-language.webp "Visual language")

## Solution

Onboarding walks people through sign-up or login, an optional referral code, and choosing a starting level of beginner, intermediate or advanced. Anyone unsure can take a short quiz to find out.

![Onboarding screens](/work/knoweb3/onboarding-screens.webp "Onboarding and level selection")

The home page works as a dashboard. It shows enrolled courses in a horizontal scroll, a weekly streak tracker that earns KNO points, and the latest Web3 articles. In Learn, courses are shown as cards with how long each takes. Inside a course, a segmented picker switches between sections such as overview, resources and practice, and a progress bar shows how much is done and how many quizzes are left. Lessons come as text or video, can be downloaded for offline use, and end with a quiz. A "Try it yourself" section suggests real tasks like setting up a crypto wallet or making a first NFT.

{% gallery columns=2 %}
![Learn: enrolled, available and completed courses](/work/knoweb3/screen-learn.webp)

![Lesson with video, transcript and a quiz](/work/knoweb3/screen-lesson.webp)
{% /gallery %}

Community holds threads, direct chat and a Q&A tab for clearing doubts. The profile collects downloads, course certificates and the NFT collection, with an option to connect a wallet. Points earned from courses and quizzes can be spent on NFTs, which then appear in the collection.

{% gallery columns=3 %}
![Community threads](/work/knoweb3/screen-community.webp)

![Profile with collection and certificates](/work/knoweb3/screen-profile.webp)

![Redeeming NFTs with KNO points](/work/knoweb3/screen-redeem-nfts.webp)
{% /gallery %}

## Outcome & learnings

We delivered a researched concept with an information architecture, user flow, wireframes, a small visual identity and high-fidelity screens for every main section. It wasn't built or tested with users, so I can't speak to how well it works in practice.

The research shaped the product more than anything else. Opportunities in the journey map turned into features almost one to one, and MoSCoW helped us say no to things that would have made it feel like a developer tool. If I took it further, I'd test the onboarding and level quiz with beginners first, since that's where the journey map showed people giving up. I'd also want to check whether points and NFTs actually help people finish courses, or just add another thing to understand.
